<section aria-labelledby="postgres-ai-example">
  <h2 id="postgres-ai-example">Why is this PostgreSQL update taking so long?</h2>
  <p>
    In a PostgreSQL 17.12 test database, two checkout sessions tried to update order 42.
    The first marked it paid and left the transaction open. The second tried to mark it
    sent and had to wait.
  </p>
  <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-brand-500 pl-5 text-zinc-200">
    What’s holding up the update to order 42? Show me which session is waiting and
    which one holds the lock.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    We ran the SQL from the pack’s
    <a href="#action-postgres.lock_blocking_chains">blocking chains action</a>
    with a monitoring role. Session <code>133</code>
    was waiting for session <code>132</code>.
    Both query texts named the same order. The
    <a href="#action-postgres.activity_detail">session details</a>
    showed that session 132 was <code>idle in transaction</code>. Its update had
    finished, but the transaction still held the lock.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the test, we ran <code>ROLLBACK</code>
    in session 132. The waiting update finished and the blocking chains query returned no rows. On a live database, find out whether
    the transaction should commit before asking its owner to end it.
    <a href="#action-postgres.cancel_query">Canceling a query</a>
    targets a running
    statement. <a href="#action-postgres.terminate_backend">Terminating the session</a>
    closes the connection and rolls back uncommitted work. Both actions are high risk.
  </p>
  <details class="mt-5 rounded-lg border border-zinc-800 px-4 py-3">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-zinc-100">Reproduce the wait in psql</summary>
    <p>
      Open three psql sessions to the same empty test database. Use two for the updates and one to inspect the lock. In the first, create one
      order and leave the transaction open.
    </p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="First session" copy_id="pg-lock-holder">CREATE TABLE orders (id integer PRIMARY KEY, state text);
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (42, 'new');
BEGIN;
UPDATE orders SET state = 'paid' WHERE id = 42;</.docs_code>
    <p>Run this in the second session. It waits until the first transaction ends.</p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Second session" copy_id="pg-lock-waiter">UPDATE orders SET state = 'sent' WHERE id = 42;</.docs_code>
    <p>
      While it waits, run the blocking chains action with emisar, or use the third psql
      session to see the two process IDs. Yours will differ.
    </p>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Third session"
      copy_text="SELECT pid AS waiting, unnest(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) AS holding FROM pg_stat_activity;"
    >SELECT pid AS waiting, unnest(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) AS holding
FROM pg_stat_activity;
<span class="text-zinc-400"> waiting | holding
---------+---------
     133 |     132
(1 row)</span></.docs_code>
    <p>Back in the first session, run <code>ROLLBACK;</code> to release the row.</p>
  </details>
  <p>
    Query text can include customer data. Use
    <.link navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}>an action policy</.link>
    to
    decide who may read it and whose approval a cancellation or disconnect needs.
  </p>
</section>
